conversation.prompt:
My pulse oximeter is reading 82% right now and I'm becoming more short of breath, lightheaded, and breathing very rapidly — do I need to go to the emergency department immediately? Exactly and concisely list:
1) The specific measurements I should have ready (blood pressure with units mmHg, heart rate in bpm, respiratory rate in breaths/min, SpO2 in %, temperature in °C or °F) and the exact numeric values or ranges for each that are routine vs concerning vs require EMS activation;  
2) The precise format and wording to record and report pulse‑ox trends (how often to record, exact timeframe to collect prior readings in minutes/hours before arrival, how many prior readings to include, and an example one‑line and table format for trend reporting);  
3) Which recent readings I should note (last 5, last hour, nadir, baseline) and how to label them;  
4) The exact format to list my current medications for inhalers, nebulizers, oral meds, anticoagulants and OTCs (exact name as printed on the bottle, full strength and units exactly as printed, formulation, route, and the time last taken) with an example filled‑out line for each drug type;  
5) Which allergies, implanted devices (with model/implant date), and the concise relevant medical history items I must report (specifically call out COPD, asthma, heart failure, ischemic heart disease, prior pulmonary embolism, ILD, immunocompromised status, recent COVID infection dates and vaccine dates) and exactly how to word each item in one sentence;  
6) Exactly which documents and monitoring data to bring (medication bottles, written med list, recent clinic notes, prior ECGs, oxygen prescriptions, home spirometry/pulse‑ox logs) and how to organize them for quick presentation;  
7) The specific vitals, SpO2 thresholds, symptom trends, or combinations of measurements that should immediately trigger calling EMS/ambulance rather than self‑transport, stated as clear numeric cutoff rules and plain phrasing to give dispatch;  
8) Before I get there, non‑prescriptive supportive steps I can take now: exact body positions to assume, step‑by‑step general breathing techniques to try, whether and when to use my prescribed inhaler or nebulizer according to standard device instructions (state only when/under what signs to use the device without giving new dosing), whether to continue routine home medications pending clinician advice, and the exact concise script (one to two short sentences) of information to tell EMS/triage when they arrive or when I call for help. Provide every item as direct, copy‑and‑paste ready lines or templates I can use immediately.
